/**

@page peano_parallelisation Parallelisation

Peano naturally uses domain decomposition to parallelise a code. 
Domain decomposition is used for both MPI and multithreading.
To enable MPI and shared memory parallelisation you have to configure your
build with --with-mpi and --with-multithreading.
Some codes and extensions (such as ExaHyPE or Swift) add tasking on top to
improve the shared memory parallelisation.


Peano's non-overlapping
multiscale domain decomposition based upon space-filling curves (SFC).
To use this scheme, you have to specify an appropriate load balancing scheme.
Without any load balancing, the code will never split up the domain and thus
effectively run serially.

@image html domain-decomposition.png

Once you use the domain decomposition, Peano's postprocessing scripts will 
append an additional  unknown value to each dof.
This last one holds the index of the underlying subpartition.
Unfortunately, this index is not persistent over multiple plots, i.e. the number might change
from plot to plot.
It only provides the information of the domain decomposition at one point.


As we use the same domain decomposition for both shared memory and MPI, it 
makes logically no difference how you configuration your MPI run: For N nodes
with T cores, you can use N ranks with T threads each. You can also use 
2N ranks and T/2 threads and so forth. 
Logically, these setups will yield the same or very similar domain decompositions.
We however see that the physical splitting has a severe impact on the runtime.
It is unfortunately depending on the simulation type, i.e. some trial and error
is required to find a good configuration per machine and simulation.


The more subpartition (trees) you allow Peano to host per rank, the more you
stress the MPI subsystem.
Many trees give Peano more headspace to balance out the mesh, but every tree
may (in theory) exchange data with any other tree, and this can quickly
overwhelm MPI.
If you encounter deadlocks, it is therefore reasonable to constrain the number
of trees (subpartitions) per rank.


 */
 
 